analysisWe need to produce an assert statement matching the comment.

We have method add(int index, E obj). It adds element at given index before existing element. The comment:

"// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then if the object is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index equals the object."

Interpretation: Precondition: index between 0 and initial size inclusive (i.e., old size). After addition, at that index the element equals obj (or both null). So we need to assert postcondition.

We need to refer to old size: \old(this.size()) or maybe this.size() before addition? Use \old(this.size()).

Also need to refer to element at that index after addition: this.get(index). Use this.get(index). The condition: if obj == null then this.get(index) == null else this.get(index).equals(obj). Could express as (obj == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(obj)). In assert syntax, we can write: (obj == null => this.get(index) == null) && (obj != null => this.get(index).equals(obj)). But also need premise that index between 0 and old size inclusive. So overall: assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ((obj == null => this.get(index) == null) && (obj != null => this.get(index).equals(obj)));

But note that after addition size increased by 1. The index is valid in new list as well (since we inserted). So we can assert that.

Thus final assert statement:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ((obj == null => this.get(index) == null) && (obj != null => this.get(index).equals(obj)));

Make sure parentheses correct: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ((obj == null => this.get(index) == null) && (obj != null => this.get(index).equals(obj)));

Return as code block.

